Public domain

Everything under this directory comes from OurAirports (https://ourairports.com/),
taken from the GitHub repository davidmegginson/ourairports-data at commit
9e51f13487de777bdc473a37d271981a2d0b30ca (2026-08-31T01:53:13Z).

OurAirports releases its data to the public domain. The terms of use on its
download page (https://ourairports.com/data/) say:

  All data is released to the Public Domain, and comes with no guarantee of
  accuracy or fitness for use.

and credit is asked for but not required:

  We'd love you to give us credit, like we give credit to our sources, but
  you're not required to.

If you give it: Data from OurAirports (https://ourairports.com/).

The repository's own LICENSE file is The Unlicense, which speaks only of
software. The public domain statement for the data is the one on the site.
The site also credits the sources it drew on (FAA, DAFIF, GeoNames and
others); whether every value taken from them could be placed in the public
domain rests on the site's statement and was not checked further.

The files under csv/ are the commit's own, byte for byte: each was compared
with the git blob the commit names for it, and csv/MANIFEST.sha256 records
the sha256, the blob and the size of each.

The files under parquet/ were made from them, and three changes were made:

  1. the header names lose the spaces airport-comments.csv puts after its
     commas
  2. airports and navaids gain a point geometry (WGS 84) from longitude_deg
     and latitude_deg
  3. the rows are sorted by id, countries and regions by code

Columns are typed by name: id and *ref as integers, *_ft, *_khz, lighted and
closed as integers, *_deg, *_degT and *_mhz as floats, everything else as the
text of the CSV. No value was changed.

The code that fetches, checks and converts is MIT, in the repository this
dataset was built from (https://github.com/yuiseki/ourairports-2026-08), and
does not apply here.
